// A320 landing-configuration poses.
//
// The model carries a 28 second animation with 248 channels driving the
// gear, its doors, the flaps, the ailerons, the air brakes and the
// engines. It cannot play: this app pins viewer.clock.shouldAnimate to
// false so the sun holds still, and Cesium advances model animations off
// that clock. Unpinning it to get the gear down would move the sun, which
// is a worse trade than it sounds for a scene graded for a specific hour.
//
// An aircraft on a five minute final does not need the cycle anyway. It
// needs ONE configuration, held: gear down, flaps out. So the animation
// is baked here as poses sampled along it, and applied through
// nodeTransformations, which is the same route the helicopter's rotors
// take and touches no clock.
//
// 83 of the 248 channels actually move; the rest are constant and are
// left to the model's own defaults. The source keyframes are baked at
// about 34 fps, 92,000 of them, which is 3.6 MB of redundancy. These are
// eight samples across the 28 seconds, 83 KB.
//
// Pick the one that reads as a landing configuration:
//   window.__isr_a320_pose('28.17')     // or '0', '4', '8' ...
//   window.__isr_a320_pose()            // list them
//
// Clip B will want two of these rather than one, since a go-around
// retracts the gear.

